About a week ago I posted a message called "This Might Be The One!"

And I'm happy to say, it was!  This was my first time making it past
the valley of death, and I must say, without weird challenges, the
second half of the game is FAR easier than the first.

I'll try to keep this brief(ish) since I'm sure nobody reads these
things all the way through.

On the whole it was a very item-poor game.  There were no magic
markers, no non-castle wishes except for a single magic lamp that I
never used, no Grey Dragon Scales, no wands of polymorph, no scrolls
of genocide (but two from thrones), no bags of holding, very few
scrolls of enchant armor, and even a plethora of little nuisances like
no scrolls of earth beyond the two in Sokoban (makes it hard to plug
up those polymorph traps) and no wands of enlightenment, but
nonetheless I persevered and after 3 years of playing them, I finally
ascended a Valkyrie named Shera.  I guess next up will be a Wizard. 
My game had two spellbooks of finger of death in the first ten levels.
 I think the RNG was taunting me.

The game started in pretty standard fashion.  I kept my dog until I
found an altar, which happened to be on Dlvl 1.  I fought my way down
to minetown, following a strategy of keeping my experience level equal
to the deepest dungeon level I'd been to, and once I made it to
Minetown, I dipped for Excalibur when the guards weren't looking.

Sokoban had an amulet of reflection, which came in handy when a
Minesend polytrap turned a gnome into a Black Dragon.  The Black
Dragon left scales, and I got myself some armor, and I was feeling
pretty good about the game all of a sudden.  This feeling lasted about
five minutes, because after I found the luckstone I found... a Master
Mindflayer and a Master Lich just standing next to each other in the
maze parts of the level.  Probably talking about who was going to get
me first.

Let this be a lesson to you, kids, put boulders over polytraps.

I didn't have a light source, so I found the two of them by running
into them and getting attacked.  Got sucked and cursed then I read a
?oTeleport... then another... then another until I was close enough to
the upstair to make a dash for it.

So first and, as it would turn out, worst disaster averted, I was
looking pretty good.  My AC was well below zero, I had displacement
and reflection, I'd collected most of the resistances by that point,
and I had an OK sword.  I fought my way down to the door of the castle
before turning around to raise my level up to 14.

An incubus on lvl 4 who I #called Don Juan boosted me from lvl 12 to
lvl 17. Sigh... such stamina!

The quest was predictably easy... except for when I walked into lava
with no levitation... my biggest YASmove of the game.  That wiped out
3 scrolls of charging which would have been VERY useful later on. 
Nothing else of much importance was lost, however, since I'd left most
of my equipment in a locked box sitting on an Elbereth square by an
altar, as I had no bag of holding, and so couldn't lug that much
around.

So by the time I was ready to take on the castle, I had Excalibur
(also had found Trollsbane, Dragonbane, and Sunsword, just about
killing my hope to wish for Magicbane) AC of -20 or so after generous
donations to the minetown priest, and dragging every piece of armor
out of the mines and Ludios.  I had no magic resistance and no bag of
holding, both of which were seriously beginning to bother me.  I think
reflection, MR, and a bag of holding are so important, that the game
should do something to insure that all three are provided by the
castle.  I was lucky and the lack of MR before I got my wishes, but
that was just luck, something bad could have happened.

Actually... something bad did happen, there were two polymorph traps
in the level above the castle.  When I went back up to get my
equipment ready for the castle run, I managed to find both of them the
hard way.  First, of course, I turned into a Black Dragon, losing my
BDSM and much of the rest of my armor, a few steps later, I turned
into a Jabberwocky, which wasn't so bad.  I put on some extra armor
I'd had laying around, I took my sword and I cleared out the castle
mostly in that form.

I found speed boots in the castle, and my 5 wand wishes gave me a BoH,
GDSM, Gauntlets of Power, an Oilskin Cloak, and (stupidly) a T-Shirt

I *REALLY* should have used that last wish for a magic marker.  A
T-Shirt is only useful if you have so many scrolls of enchant armor
that you can't enchant anything else any further, and that definitely
was not the case for me.

So then I began the process of putting the finishing touches on my AK.
 I scoured the dungeon for any more scrolls of enchant armor, I
identified everything, I collected tins of tengu, I sacrificed for
Mojo, and made holy water.

When all was said and done, I was Lvl 22, I had AC -22, and a pretty
well rounded AK, with +7 Mojo, +5 Silver Saber, and +0 Sunsword, which
I never used, since I found a magic lamp in the valley of the dead and
just kept it lit.

Gehennom was shockingly easy.  Asmodeus decided to throw a little
party and invite Dispater and Yeenoghu, but I engraved Elbereth (a
tactic I employed liberally throughout the game) and bounced my hammer
off of them.  There were a *lot* of liches.  I had genocided standard
liches and standard mindflayers with thrones, since those had given me
lots of trouble the last time I made it to the castle, and I figured I
wouldn't run into the meaner variety until I had lots of equipment and
could handle them, which was pretty much right, as it turned out.  But
still I killed 5 arch liches, and 11 master liches, which seems like a
lot.

The final run was made INFINITELY easier by the fact that I'd taken
the time to dig a 2-square wide tunnel from the upstair to the
downstair of every single level in the entire dungeon.  I never got
stuck when making the run back up, and could in general just dodge
monsters.  I had two wands of death with 8 total shots in them, and
this was good for 4 Wizard slayings (I did a fifth by hand, just to
prove to him I wasn't a wimp)

The planes were pretty easy, though I spent the orb of fate, and two
standard crystal balls finding the portals.  Next time I will
DEFINITELY raise my INT to the max if I plan on using this method to
find the portals.  Air elementals are rough, and I still don't know
what was going on with the mechanics on the plane of water.

I used conflict on the astral plane.  Death is a bastard, dropped my
max HP by fifty.  The second altar (center) was the correct one, and
Tyr got his birthday present.

